	"about": "How Indonesian verb forms are made and turned into lessons. Written by Claude (Opus) with Ben on 17 November 2026, the twelfth language and the last. Like Chinese, the verb never changes for who or for when, so `built` makes each form from a pattern: sudah in front for done, sedang for going on, akan for later, tidak for not. The one thing that does change is the command: a verb that wears meN- (menulis, membeli, mengambil) takes it off to tell someone to do it (Tulis! Beli! Ambil!), while ber- verbs and bare roots stay as they are (Duduk! Bekerja!). `roots` finds what meN- was put on: the dump's own 'active of buat' where it says so, and otherwise the candidate the meN- sound rules offer (men- before a vowel was t-, mem- was p-, meng- was k-, meny- was s-) that the dump knows best as a word. `of` corrects the few the rules get wrong, and `keep` lists the meN- verbs that do not drop it, because nothing is done to anything (Menangislah, not Tangis). There is no reading to show: the language is written in the Latin alphabet.",
